A||||14||fierosound||engine removal - how to...||||5
Z||000000||fierosound||11-24-1999||11:54 AM||||It seems most auto places here know almost nothing about Fieros.  I'm not sure if I can trust them.  I think I'll have to consider replacing the engine myself.  Just a couple questions to those who've done it.<P>1 - What are you using to raise the car high enough to drop the engine cradle and pull it out from under the car?<P>2 - Are you using a wheeled dolly of some type for the cradle?  Is this something you built or bought?<P>Thanks in advance...<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-24-1999||12:07 PM||||I made a dolly from some casters 4x4s and plywood- for the hoist I just threw some extra support under the rafters in the garage and used a come-along to raise the car. Took off the rear facia put a bar through the rear bumper support and hooked it to the come-along ( after you raise the car use jack stands of course) ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sluppy123||11-24-1999||12:45 PM||||I made a dolly from some plans I'd seen in a Fiero mag. It's made of 2x4's and 4 casters.<BR>To raise the body off the engine cradle <BR>I used 2 15" floor jacks and some blocks of wood to get the car high enough to clear the struts. (I think you need about 36"? from the floor to the highest point of the wheel well to clear the struts)then roll the cradle assembly out through the wheel well.took me about 6 hours from start to finish.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||11-24-1999||03:37 PM||||I have a dolly made from 2" angle iron with 4 castors.  I use a 20" floor jack with a piece of 2x6 lumber that spans the width of the car just forward of the cradle mount.  This will lift the car just enough so the engine will clear the trunk floorpan.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000004||dogtired||11-24-1999||03:49 PM||||Been there Done that.<BR>I didn't even approach local shops, figured that as a waste of time.<BR>As described in one of the manuals, I used the 4X4 method.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Literally took a 4X4 fence post 8 foot long, put it under the car where the jack pads are. Used a hydraulic jack on one end and a fiero jack on the other. (then I used jack stands) It will worry you a little, the front will come up some but not off the ground, make sure it (the front) is blocked GOOD. My motor is a V6 and I pulled the Plenum, distributor and dogbone mount off while it was still in the car. Then raised the body enough to pull the motor out through the right wheel well.<BR>Not to start over here but, my method was to unhook most everything at first, remove rear tires/wheels and lower everything to set on the 4 wheel cart. Then remove the cradle bolts and jack the car body up off the motor. I wouldn't raise up the car and then try and lower the cradle on to the cart?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>I also made my own 4 wheel cart. Saw recently where one of our friends from the Netherlands used a pallet jack, thought that was a great idea.<BR>All the books seem to infer that it is quite difficult? My opinion on a scale of difficulity from 1 to 10 where 1 is the easiest. I would give it a 6 1/2. Just do not get in a hurry! GOOD Luck.||||reg||6
Z||000005||DJRice||11-24-1999||04:08 PM||||Yup, I use a 4x4 and two floor jacks.  The best way to handle it is just disconnect all wiring/hoses/cables first.  Also, go ahead and break loose the 4 bolts that hold the cradle to the car.  Then set car down so that the cradle is on a dolly of some sort.  Next finish disconnecting the cradle, dog bone, suspension, etc.  Then raise the car and the engine will stay on the ground.  Voila!<P>You can get it done in about 1 day if you work all day straight.<P>Good luck and be careful.  Always make sure that the front wheels are blocked up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||11-24-1999||05:23 PM||||You can also pull the enging from the top||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sage||11-24-1999||07:35 PM||||Look up v8-Archies' site.  He has detailed instructions on how he removes them. I have done it a couple different ways.  I like Phils' idea never tried that one. If you take the 3 bolts that hold the struts to the tower out, you can push the struts down far enough to get a bracket (I used a piece of 3x4x1/4" angle iron the width of the large opening with holes drilled to run bolts through) up through the large opening and attached a chain to the bracket(with grade8 bolts) then hoisted the car with the chain from a 6,000 lb rating engine hoist.  Then you block the car up with jack-stands or 6x6's or something solid!  Then use the same hoist to lower the cradle onto a dolly made of 2x4's and steel castors.  Another time for a dolly, I took my engine stand apart and just used the base. It has 3"spokes" and great castors, worked really well and the cradle fits nicely on it.  Just some suggestions.  I recomend checking out v8-Archies site.  Anybody got the URL?||||reg||1
Z||000008||William Federle||11-24-1999||08:16 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierosound||11-25-1999||12:02 AM||||Thanks for all the input guys...  I will file this for the near future when it will come in handy!!||||reg||10
Z||000010||RZURCTN||11-26-1999||01:37 AM||||Don't 8-ball me for this, but I have hooked a hoist right to the trunk latch and raised the car.  This was without the cradle in of course.  I don't think that I would lift it that way with the engine in.  But it is an easy way to move the car if you need to.  Plus it keeps the nose down.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||11-28-1999||12:25 AM||||I just talked to a mechanic about rebuilding my motor.  He said that, if I bring the motor to him, he would rebuild it (for $1500!), but he refused to reomove the motor himself.  I've heard the same thing from every mechanic I've talked to.  I'm not a mechanic, but I guess I'm gonna have to do the whole thing myself whenever I get around to it.  Oh, well.  I need to save the extra money anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000012||fierospeeder||11-28-1999||01:12 AM||||what would be the difference to the mechanic from removing a front wheel drive engine or from a van?||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||11-30-1999||01:40 AM||||Don't really know.  One mechanic said that all of the bolts were rusted together on the last one he did.  You'd think that wouldn't be a problem.  Bust 'em loose and replace 'em.  I suppose mechanics are a little scared because most people drop the cradle and the mechanics around here aren't sure how to do that.  I guess, if they don't know what they are doing, I'd rather do it myself anyway.  (Not that I know what I'm doing, but I can ask you guys without bruising my ego!!)||||reg||1
Z||000014||DJRice||11-30-1999||09:04 AM||||Actually I just snatched the V6 out of an 85 SE over the holiday weekend in less than 6 hours with a cherry picker, a 4x4, and some blocks.  I picked the whole car up with the hoist via the rings on the engine, and put it on jack stands.  Then removed all wiring, hoses, shifter and throttle cables, fuel lines, etc. Then loosed cradle bolts.  Let car back down and centered the cradle on top of a dolly.  Removed the bolts that hold the struts in place, brake lines, and cradle bolts.  Then I just hooked the hoist to the trunk latch loop thing and Voila.  Engine out.<P>I used the blocks and 4x4 to prop the car up once the engine was free.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by DJRice (edited 11-30-1999).]||||reg||1
